--TEST--
an in-process request made from inside one is refused at the door, naming why — ADR 0079 § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The edge of the sibling cases: what answers a synthetic request is the
// program's own entry, so a top level that asks for one is also the code that
// runs to answer it. Without this refusal a program would answer its own
// request forever, and `rule:errors/on-limit`'s script-depth ceiling would report an
// engine limit rather than the mistake.
//
// The message crosses the boundary the long way round, which is the point: the
// child catches the refusal and writes it as its response body, so the parent
// -- whose own call succeeded -- reads out what its child was told.
try {
    var $answered = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/anything");
    echo $answered->body(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message;
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::request could not run the request: an in-process request may not be made from inside one — the program answering it is the same program that asked, so a second request would answer itself
